To create a link for every post, you'll need to add a new location block to your existing configuration. Since you're hosting your website using HTML files, you'll use the `location` block to catch the URL pattern for your posts.

Assuming your post URLs will follow a consistent pattern, such as `/post/[post-id]`, where `[post-id]` is a unique identifier for each post, you can use a regular expression to match these URLs.

Here's an example of how you can add this configuration:

```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    listen [::]:443 ssl http2;
    server_name www.askfundu.com;
    root /usr/share/nginx/html;

    # ... existing configuration ...

    # Post link generation
    location ~ ^/post/([0-9]+)$ {
        rewrite ^/post/([0-9]+)$ /post-$1.html break;
    }
}
```

This configuration uses the `location` block to match URLs that start with `/post/` followed by one or more digits (captured in the `([0-9]+)` group). The `rewrite` directive is used to redirect the request to a new URL, replacing the `/post/` part with `/post-` and appending the captured group (`$1`) to the end. For example, if the URL is `/post/123`, the rewritten URL would be `/post-123.html`.

This configuration assumes that your post files are stored in the same directory as your main HTML files, in a file named `post-[post-id].html`, where `[post-id]` is the same value as the captured group in the URL.

You'll need to create the `post-*.html` files for each post and place them in the correct directory.

Note that this configuration is case-sensitive, so if you want to make it case-insensitive, you can use the `~*` prefix instead of `~` in the `location` block.

Also, don't forget to reload your Nginx configuration after making changes to ensure the new configuration is applied.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

