To add an object of a specific class type to an `ArrayList` in Java, you can do the following:

1. Create an instance of the class:
```java
MyClass obj = new MyClass();
```
Replace `MyClass` with the actual class name of the type you want to add.

2. Create an `ArrayList` of the same type:
```java
ArrayList<MyClass> myList = new ArrayList<>();
```
3. Add the object to the `ArrayList` using the `add()` method:
```java
myList.add(obj);
```
Here's a complete example:
```java
public class MyClass {
    private String name;

    public MyClass(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<MyClass> myList = new ArrayList<>();

        MyClass obj1 = new MyClass("John");
        MyClass obj2 = new MyClass("Jane");

        myList.add(obj1);
        myList.add(obj2);

        // Print the list
        for (MyClass obj : myList) {
            System.out.println(obj.getName());
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we create an `ArrayList` of `MyClass` objects and add two instances of `MyClass` to it. Then, we print the names of the objects in the list using a for-each loop.

Note that when you add an object to an `ArrayList`, it is not a copy of the object, but a reference to the original object. This means that if you modify the object after adding it to the list, the changes will be reflected in the list as well.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

